The speaker then broadens the rule beyond any one industry. He says it does not matter what industry one goes into; if a person is honest, authentic, and genuine, everyone knows that. The book should keep this as a character-and-trust claim, not as a guaranteed success formula: $\{\text{honesty},\ \text{authenticity},\ \text{genuineness}\}
\longrightarrow
\text{recognized character}.$

